---
/**
 * What the site covers, how it is sourced, and its limits.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { sources } from "../lib/sources";

const sourceCount = Object.keys(sources).length;
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="An index of AI capability, adoption, governance and safety. Scope, sourcing, limits and construction."
  cites={[]}
>
  <section class="section section--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="masthead">
        <h1>About</h1>
        <p class="masthead__meta mono">
          {sourceCount} sources
          <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
          zero client JavaScript
          <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
          every figure cited
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="doc">
        <p class="lede">
          Data on AI is published by many organisations in different formats. This site collects
          those figures into a single index and records the origin of each one. Sources include
          Epoch AI, Eurostat, OpenAlex, Microsoft and government publishers.
        </p>

        <h2>Scope</h2>
        <p>
          The site covers benchmark results, training compute and disclosed cost, adoption by
          population and by enterprise, published frontier safety frameworks, research volume,
          reported incidents, and the law and policy that applies to AI in eleven jurisdictions.
        </p>
        <p>
          Four datasets are compiled here from primary documents: the Governance Readiness Index,
          the AI Law and Policy Index, the Frontier Safety Framework Index and the Compute
          Threshold Index. The <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> sets out how each is scored.
        </p>

        <h2>Sourcing</h2>
        <p>
          Each source is declared once with its licence, attribution, update cadence and stated
          limitations. Each processed record carries a source identifier. Licence and attribution
          are resolved from that registry when a page is built. The pipeline does not write data
          whose licence forbids republication, and the site does not render it. Removing a source
          from the registry removes everything derived from it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every figure carries its source, licence and retrieval date. The
          <a href="/sources/">sources page</a> lists each dataset in use, its licence, its use here
          and its stated limitations.
        </p>

        <h2>Limits</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            The site publishes no forecasts of its own. Straight-line extrapolations appear on the
            progress page only, and each is labelled with its fit, its window and the direction of
            its bias.
          </li>
          <li>
            Governance readiness records the machinery a jurisdiction has. It is not an assessment
            of quality or enforcement, and a jurisdiction may score low by choice.
          </li>
          <li>Nothing here is legal advice. See the <a href="/terms/">terms</a>.</li>
          <li>
            There are no accounts, comments, newsletter, advertising or revenue model, and no
            tracking beyond aggregate page counts.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Construction</h2>
        <p>
          A Python pipeline fetches and processes each source. A static site generator renders the
          result. Charts and maps are drawn to SVG when the site is built, so pages carry no client
          JavaScript. Data is refreshed daily. A dataset is rewritten only when its records change.
        </p>
        <p>AI was used to help build this website.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
